AB  - Sedimentation in sediment-laden rivers degrades reservoir and channel capacities. To achieve coordinated reservoir-river sediment regulation while meeting multiple water demands, this study develops a synergetics-based water-sediment multi-objective scheduling model considering reservoir-river coupling in the Middle and Lower Yellow River. Pareto trade-off analysis examines competition and synergy among subsystems. Changes in sediment transport targets and sensitivity analyses reveal the system's response characteristics. Results indicate: (1) Sustaining channel sediment transport capacity requires a bankfull discharge above 4000 m3/s; (2) The model achieves an annual mean sediment delivery to the sea of 1.992 × 108 t, and the reservoir-river coupled sediment transport subsystem competes with the other subsystems; (3) Changing sediment release requirements shifts reservoir operation trajectories and alters ecological, water-supply, and hydropower benefits; (4) The system is sensitive to inflow and sediment variability, and a 50% inflow reduction markedly weakens multi-objective performance. The results provide insights for sediment-laden cascade reservoir management.
