AB  - Effective wastewater treatment has gained significant momentum due to limited global clean water access. Although many models for wastewater treatment plants (WWTPs) exist, the integration of byproduct and waste management systems remains limited, potentially incurring increased costs and pollutant emissions. Moreover, existing optimization approaches inadequately capture the uncertainty of the treatment process effectiveness, which compromises the system's ability to satisfy quality requirements. Thus, a target-oriented robust optimization (TORO) model for a hybrid WWTP and byproduct disposal system (BDS) that minimizes cost and environmental impact is developed. The case study implemented validates the model's ability to balance objective trade-offs while underscoring the importance of the process effectiveness on the system's performance. TORO allows decision makers to set targets and select solutions for managing hybrid WWTP-BDS systems based on their varying risk-appetites. These solutions show increased stability, demonstrating the potential of the proposed framework to enhance sustainability and robustness of wastewater management.
