AB  - Accurate runoff prediction is essential for water resource management. This study developed a residual-informed hybrid physics-informed machine learning (PIML) framework that integrates Xin'anjiang (XAJ) hydrological model, Seasonal-Trend decomposition using Loess (STL), Variational Mode Decomposition (VMD), Convolutional Neural Network–Long Short-Term Memory (CNN–LSTM) hybrid architecture, and SHapley Additive Explanations (SHAP)—and applies it to a representative karst basin in Southwest China. When evaluated for runoff prediction, the framework achieves a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.93 and reduces peak prediction error to 8.14%, outperforming standalone models. SHAP-based interpretability analysis reveals soil moisture, cumulative atmospheric humidity, and surface energy balance as key drivers. When coupled with a reservoir operation model, the proposed framework produces more accurate inflow estimates, thereby enhancing hydropower generation performance under challenging hydrological conditions. Overall, the proposed framework enhances runoff prediction accuracy, demonstrates strong engineering applicability, and provides transferable solutions for water resources management in complex karst basins.
