AB  - Evaluating long-term dust storm mitigation strategies such as terrain modification remains challenging due to fragmented analytical workflows and the absence of integrated tools for iterative, scenario-driven analysis. This paper presents an interactive web-based framework for scenario-driven analysis of dust storm variability and mitigation strategies. The framework integrates terrain editing, HPC-based simulation management, and comparative visualization within a unified environment, enabling domain scientists to draw terrain modification polygons directly on a geographic map, automatically update simulation input files, submit and monitor simulations asynchronously, and compare results through linked visualization views. A case study focused on the Arabian Peninsula showcases the framework’s ability to reveal new insights into dust storm variability and assess the effectiveness of mitigation strategies. Evaluation with five independent domain experts yields a mean System Usability Scale (SUS) score of 82.0, and all participants confirmed the efficiency of the integrated workflow. This work represents a shift from passive data visualization to an active, hypothesis-driven research paradigm to support complex environmental decision-making.
