AB  - Conventional stationary models fail to account for temporal variability and dynamic factors influencing flood regimes, limiting their effectiveness in flood risk prediction. This study introduces a Non-Stationary Multi-Covariate Flood Frequency Analysis (NSMCFFA) framework to improve flood risk modeling under changing climatic conditions. Applied to five gauging stations in the Narmada River Basin (NRB), India, the framework integrates climatic, ecological, and anthropogenic covariates. We also present TRUE (Time-varying Return level for Univariate Extremes), a MATLAB-based GUI that allows user-defined covariates and employs the Deviance Information Criterion (DIC) for optimal model selection. Bayesian inference is used to estimate model parameters and return levels. Results reveal spatial variability in covariate influence: Mohgaon is climate-driven, while Barmanghat, Handia, Garudeshwar, and Mandleshwar are shaped by both climatic and anthropogenic factors. Findings show that non-stationary models better represent flood return levels than stationary ones, establishing NSMCFFA as a robust framework for flood management in the NRB.
