AB  - This study refines the adversarial generation of extreme samples (ExGAN) framework to generate extreme rainfall scenarios over the Korean Peninsula based on 5 km gridded observations (2014–2023). Methodological refinements include separating typhoon and monsoon data, applying log transformation to stabilize heavy-tailed distributions, using data-driven mean residual life plots for objective generalized Pareto distribution thresholding, modifying conditional sampling to correspond to physically meaningful return periods, and improving Fréchet inception distance (FID) evaluation by defining reference distributions from autoencoder-derived features of the test set. Performance is evaluated using FID, mean absolute percentage error (MAPE), and reconstruction loss. For 100-year typhoon events, the FID, MAPE, and reconstruction loss are 1.18±0.65, 14.19%, and 0.0921, respectively. Semivariogram analysis confirms that the generated extremes preserve spatial dependence patterns consistent with observations. This work presents a transferable methodological advancement for generative modeling in complex climates and a practical tool for climate risk assessment in Korea.
